# Runtime configuration — copy to `.env.local` for local dev; set the same
# names in Vercel project settings for a deploy. Every value here is a
# placeholder: never commit a real one. The app builds and the reference
# browser runs with none of them set — each variable only switches its own
# feature on.

# Neon Postgres pooled connection string, from the Neon–Vercel integration or
# the Neon Console. Without it, character data answers 503 and the character
# pages explain themselves instead of pretending you own nothing.
# DATABASE_URL=

# Neon Auth (Managed Better Auth), from the Neon Console after enabling Auth
# on the project. Without the pair, sign-in quietly degrades.
# NEON_AUTH_BASE_URL=

# You generate this one: `openssl rand -base64 32` — must be 32+ characters.
# NEON_AUTH_COOKIE_SECRET=

# The shared invite code that gates sign-up (D20). Fail-closed: with this
# unset, nobody can register at all. Hand the code to the people at the table.
# SIGNUP_INVITE_CODE=

# Sentry error reporting — optional. Not a secret (it ships in the client
# bundle by design); without it the SDK is never initialised.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=

# Sentry auth token for build-time source-map upload — optional. Vercel
# project settings only, never `.env.local`. Errors still report without it;
# their stack traces just stay minified. (SENTRY_ORG and SENTRY_PROJECT are
# only read when this is set.)
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=

# --- GitHub Actions secrets (not env-file variables) ------------------------
# The migration workflows in .github/workflows/ need these set once as
# repository secrets on GitHub, not here: NEON_API_KEY, NEON_PROJECT_ID,
# VERCEL_TOKEN, VERCEL_PROJECT_ID, VERCEL_TEAM_ID — plus DATABASE_URL as a
# secret for the production migrate job.
